Z||000008||Carrolles||11-06-2001||02:20 PM||||I think you will be defeating your purpose.  That will add back pressure, even if the cross sectional area is the same, due to the change of direction in flow caused by the cone.<P>Jet engines use this to increase the velocity of the exhaust gasses to increase thrust.  Backpressure isn't an issue there.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Borgio||11-06-2001||02:42 PM||||I was thinking the same thing as Carrolles. If I would work, I don't understand it. I don't even see how it would increase the velocity though. Cool idea though. You definitely get points for effort. Try it, you may get some points for success too!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTZ34||11-06-2001||02:58 PM||||Hugh,  I think I agree with Carrolles on the use in jet engines.  The design that was drawn actually increases volume as the distance increases from the pipe.  That is because of the increase in circumference on the cone.  In order to keep the planar volume equal, the inner cone should increase in diameter at a faster rate than the outer cone to keep the volume the same.  In order to scavenge, you want the surface area of the cone to be as large as possible while keeping reasonable velocity through cone, and exiting in a high vacuum area.  If the exhaust cone ended further up from the bottom of the car, there would be more vacuum.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
